<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp2sec6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">


    <title>The Subspace Theorem</title>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-subspace_thm">
        <title>Subspace Theorem</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m> and <m>W\subset V</m> is a subspace, then the vector space operations of <m>V</m> restricted to <m>W</m> make <m>W</m> a vector space (i.e. <m>W</m> satisfies axioms (VS1)-(VS8)).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Try a direct proof.
            For as important and useful as the Subspace Theorem is, its proof is surprisingly straightforward.
            You will need to verify each property (VS1)-(VS8).
            Most properties are immediately inherited from <m>V</m>.
            The only two that are not as immediate are (V2) and inverses (V3)
            You may find  <xref ref="prop-subspaces_contain_zero"/>,  <xref ref="prop-vector_space_inverses_scaling"/>, and  <xref ref="def-subspace"/> useful.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <p>
        The Subspace Theorem is very powerful for proving things are vector spaces quickly without having to verify (VS1)-(VS8).
        In particular, together with <xref ref="prop-vector_space_of_lists"/> and <xref ref="prop-vector_space_of_functions"/>, we can construct and verify infinitely many new vector spaces.
    </p>
    <example xml:id="ex-subspaces-calculus">
        <title>Examples of Subspaces from Calculus</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>I\subset \R</m> is an interval (possibly all of <m>\R</m>), then in your calculus courses you showed that each of the following subsets of the vector space <m>\mathcal{F}(I,\R)</m> satisfy (S0)-(S2), or in other words, is a subspace:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Continuous Functions: 
                        <me>\mathcal{C}(I,\R) = \{ f\in \mathcal{F}(I,\R) \ | \ f \text{ is continuous at each point in the domain } I\},</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Differentiable Functions: 
                        <me>\mathcal{D}(I,\R) = \{ f\in \mathcal{F}(I,\R) \ | \ f \text{ is differentiable at each point in the domain } I\},</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Integrable functions: 
                        <me>\mathcal{M}(I,\R) = \{ f\in \mathcal{F}(I,\R) \ | \ \int_I f(x)dx &lt; \infty\},</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Polynomials: 
                        <me>\mathcal{P}(\R) = \{ f\in \mathcal{F}(I,\R) \ | \ f \text{ is a polynomial}\},</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Polynomials of bounded degree: 
                        <me>\mathcal{P}_n(\R) = \{ f\in \mathcal{F}(I,\R) \ | \ f \text{ is a polynomial of degree less than or equal to } n\}.</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                The Subspace Theorem then implies these are vector spaces.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    <p>
        These and related vector spaces play important roles throughout mathematics, physics, and computer science, including in functional analysis, Fourier analysis, wave mechanics, quantum mechanics, centrality, and ranking.
    </p>

    <insight>
        
    <p>
        In Fourier analysis we look at the set of functions <m>f:\R\to \R</m> that repeat every <m>2\pi</m> (i.e. <m>f(x+2\pi)=f(x)</m>).
        Let <m>\mathcal{P}_{2\pi}(\R,\R)</m> denote this set.
        How you might show this collection of functions is a vector space?
        Explain your thinking at each step, why you make the arguments you do.
    </p>

    </insight>


</section>








